//! member: true
//!
//! std.os.users — the machine's ACCOUNTS, as its two plain files write
//! them: a user line of `passwd(5)` and a group line of `group(5)`.
//! Stability tier: core.
//!
//! **Pure parsing, and nothing else.** Every function here takes TEXT
//! the caller already holds and answers from it. Nothing in this module
//! opens a file, asks a name service (NSS, a directory server, a
//! cache), or reaches the host at all: it carries no capability and it
//! runs at comptime. The program that wants the machine's accounts
//! reads them itself and says where from —
//! `users.user_named(fs.read_text("/etc/passwd")?, "wolf")` — so the
//! file it trusted is in its own source, which is what `su`, `login`,
//! `id` and `ls -l` need and all a module without a name service can
//! honestly offer. A host that keeps its accounts somewhere else (a
//! directory service, macOS's for most of its users) is not answered by
//! these files, and this module does not pretend otherwise — the next
//! paragraph says exactly what that costs.
//!
//! **What the two files cannot see** (wolf-std#68, ruling #99 R2: the
//! limit is documented, and the fix is a builtin, not a better reader).
//! macOS keeps every ordinary user — every account System Settings
//! makes — and every local user's group memberships in its directory
//! service (Open Directory, whose local node is
//! `/var/db/dslocal/nodes/Default`). Its `/etc/passwd` and `/etc/group`
//! carry `root`, `nobody`, `daemon` and the system's `_` accounts, and
//! the password file's own header says it is "consulted directly only
//! when the system is running in single-user mode". On linux the same is
//! true of every account `nsswitch.conf` sends elsewhere: LDAP, NIS,
//! SSSD, systemd's dynamic users. For such an account each lookup here
//! answers `none`, `users` and `groups` leave it out, and `groups_of`
//! gives the primary id it was handed and nothing more — so `id NAME`,
//! `whoami`, `chown user:group`, `chgrp` and `groups` written over this
//! module say "no such user" where the host's own tools answer. bu24
//! measured it: 30 name-printing cases skip on CI's macOS runner, whose
//! own account is the directory service's, and
//! `tests/os/users/directory_accounts.lu` holds it as a row there: the
//! host names the runner's uid, `/etc/passwd` answers `none` for it by
//! id and by name, and `groups_of` misses groups the process holds.
//!
//! A pure reader cannot see a name service, so nothing here will grow
//! one. The future fix is a host account lookup — the C library's
//! `getpwnam`, `getpwuid`, `getgrnam`, `getgrgid` and `getgrouplist`,
//! which ask whatever the host is configured to ask — as wolf builtins,
//! asked of wolf-lang from wolf-std#68; when they land, host lookups ship
//! BESIDE these functions and every function here keeps its answer.
//! Until then two things still work on every host: the NUMBERS
//! (`std.env.uid`, `std.env.groups`, `std.fs.stat` answer ids whatever
//! keeps the names, and printing the number when a lookup is `none` is
//! what `id` itself does for an id no name service knows), and the
//! host's own tool, started through `std.process` — `/usr/bin/id -un`
//! is how the row above learns the name the files do not hold.
//!
//! **What it is for.** `std.env.uid` and `std.fs.stat` answer NUMBERS;
//! this is where a number gets its name and a name its number, and
//! where the three arguments of `std.process.set_ids` come from:
//! `user_named` gives the user and group ids, `groups_of` the
//! supplementary groups.
//!
//! **The two lines.**
//!
//! ```text
//! name:password:uid:gid:comment:home:shell         a user  (7 fields)
//! name:password:gid:member,member,…                a group (4 fields)
//! ```
//!
//! The fields are cut at `:` and at nothing else; none is trimmed. A
//! line is well formed when it has exactly that many fields, its name
//! is not empty and its ids are decimal. The password field is carried
//! by neither type: on every current host it is a placeholder (`x`,
//! `*`), the real one being in a file this module does not read.
//!
//! **An id** is `0..=4294967294`. A leading `-` is read the way the
//! host reads it — as that many below 4294967296 — because macOS writes
//! `nobody` as `-2`, which is 4294967294; `-1` is therefore no id (it
//! is every host call's "leave this one alone") and a line carrying it
//! is malformed.
//!
//! **Errors are rows, never traps** (D30, §2). Two tags:
//!
//! - `parse` — one line is not a user (or a group) line: `parse_user`,
//!   `parse_group`.
//! - `none` — the text holds no such account: the four lookups.
//!
//! The readers of a whole text (`users`, `groups`, the lookups,
//! `groups_of`) SKIP what is not an account line — a blank line, a `#`
//! comment (macOS's file opens with ten), a `+`/`-` compatibility
//! entry, a malformed line — instead of failing the file for it: one
//! bad line must not lock every user out, and `parse_user` is there for
//! the program that wants to report it.

/// One user: a line of `passwd(5)`, less its password field.
pub struct User {
    /// The login name. Never empty.
    name: str,
    /// The user id, `0..=4294967294`.
    uid: int,
    /// The user's own (primary) group id.
    gid: int,
    /// The comment field: usually the full name. May be empty.
    comment: str,
    /// The home directory. May be empty.
    home: str,
    /// The login shell; empty means the host's default.
    shell: str,
}

/// One group: a line of `group(5)`, less its password field.
pub struct Group {
    /// The group's name. Never empty.
    name: str,
    /// The group id, `0..=4294967294`.
    gid: int,
    /// The users written as members, in the file's order. A user whose
    /// OWN group this is (their `User.gid`) is usually not listed.
    members: List[str],
}

/// A decimal id field. Private: the one place the id rule lives.
fn id_of(field: str) -> int ! {parse} {
    var digits = field
    var negative = false
    if field.starts_with("-") {
        negative = true
        digits = field[1..]
    }
    if digits.len == 0 || digits.len > 10 {
        return parse
    }
    // Annotated: the reference machine keeps an unannotated literal
    // binding at `i32` (wolf-interp#173), and an id needs 33 bits.
    var value: int = 0
    for raw in digits.bytes() {
        let c = raw as int
        if c < 48 || c > 57 {
            return parse
        }
        value = value * 10 + (c - 48)
    }
    if negative {
        value = 4294967296 - value
    }
    if value < 0 || value > 4294967294 {
        return parse
    }
    value
}

/// Is `line` one the whole-text readers pass over without trying it: a
/// blank line, a `#` comment, a `+` or `-` compatibility entry?
fn skipped(line: str) -> bool {
    line.len == 0 || line.starts_with("#") || line.starts_with("+") || line.starts_with("-")
}

/// One user line, parsed.
///
/// Total over text and PURE — comptime-safe, reaching no host. The line
/// is taken as written, without its newline.
///
/// Rows: `parse` when `line` does not have exactly seven `:`-separated
/// fields, its name is empty, or its user or group id is not a decimal
/// id (the module header says what an id is). Never traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let u = users.parse_user("wolf:x:1000:1000:A Wolf:/home/wolf:/bin/sh")?
/// u.name == "wolf" && u.uid == 1000 && u.gid == 1000
/// u.comment == "A Wolf" && u.home == "/home/wolf" && u.shell == "/bin/sh"
/// let nobody = users.parse_user("nobody:*:-2:-2:Unprivileged User:/var/empty:/usr/bin/false")?
/// nobody.uid == 4294967294
/// ```
pub fn parse_user(line: str) -> User ! {parse} {
    let f = line.split(":")
    if f.len != 7 || f[0].len == 0 {
        return parse
    }
    let uid = id_of(f[2])?
    let gid = id_of(f[3])?
    User { name: f[0], uid: uid, gid: gid, comment: f[4], home: f[5], shell: f[6] }
}

/// One group line, parsed.
///
/// Total over text and PURE. The member list is cut at `,`; an empty
/// piece (a trailing comma, an empty list) is not a member.
///
/// Rows: `parse` when `line` does not have exactly four `:`-separated
/// fields, its name is empty, or its group id is not a decimal id.
/// Never traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let g = users.parse_group("pack:x:2000:wolf,cub")?
/// g.name == "pack" && g.gid == 2000 && g.members.len == 2 && g.members[1] == "cub"
/// let empty = users.parse_group("lone:x:2001:")?
/// empty.members.len == 0
/// ```
pub fn parse_group(line: str) -> Group ! {parse} {
    let f = line.split(":")
    if f.len != 4 || f[0].len == 0 {
        return parse
    }
    let gid = id_of(f[2])?
    var members = List[str]()
    for m in f[3].split(",") {
        if m.len > 0 {
            (mut members).push(m)
        }
    }
    Group { name: f[0], gid: gid, members: members }
}

/// Every user in `text` — the text of a `passwd(5)` file — in the
/// file's order.
///
/// Total and PURE. A line that is not a user line (blank, a `#`
/// comment, a `+`/`-` entry, malformed) is passed over; `parse_user`
/// says why one line is refused. **Every user the FILE holds, not every
/// user the host has**: on macOS the list read from `/etc/passwd` is
/// root, nobody, daemon and the `_` system accounts, never the people
/// (the module header's "What the two files cannot see").
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let all = users.users("# accounts\nroot:x:0:0:root:/root:/bin/sh\nbroken line\nwolf:x:1000:1000::/home/wolf:\n")
/// all.len == 2 && all[0].name == "root" && all[1].uid == 1000
/// ```
pub fn users(text: str) -> List[User] {
    var out = List[User]()
    for line in text.lines() {
        if skipped(line) {
            continue
        }
        let u = parse_user(line) else |_| {
            continue
        }
        (mut out).push(u)
    }
    out
}

/// Every group in `text` — the text of a `group(5)` file — in the
/// file's order.
///
/// Total and PURE. Lines that are not group lines are passed over, as
/// `users` passes them. Every group the FILE holds, with the members the
/// file writes: a group or a membership the host keeps elsewhere (macOS's
/// directory service, LDAP, NIS) is not in it.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let all = users.groups("root:x:0:\npack:x:2000:wolf,cub\n\n")
/// all.len == 2 && all[1].name == "pack" && all[1].members[0] == "wolf"
/// ```
pub fn groups(text: str) -> List[Group] {
    var out = List[Group]()
    for line in text.lines() {
        if skipped(line) {
            continue
        }
        let g = parse_group(line) else |_| {
            continue
        }
        (mut out).push(g)
    }
    out
}

/// The FIRST user in `text` named `name` — what `su name` looks up.
///
/// PURE. The first line wins, as it does for the host's own reader.
///
/// Rows: `none` when no user line carries that name — which is also
/// the answer for an account the host keeps outside the file (macOS's
/// directory service, LDAP, NIS), however well the host knows it; the
/// module header says what that costs `su`, `chown` and `id`. Never
/// traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let text = "root:x:0:0:root:/root:/bin/sh\nwolf:x:1000:1000::/home/wolf:/bin/sh\n"
/// let u = users.user_named(text, "wolf")?
/// u.uid == 1000 && u.home == "/home/wolf"
/// let missing = users.user_named(text, "cub") else |_| users.parse_user("none:x:7:7:::")?
/// missing.uid == 7
/// ```
pub fn user_named(text: str, name: str) -> User ! {none} {
    for u in users(text) {
        if u.name == name {
            return u
        }
    }
    return none
}

/// The FIRST user in `text` whose id is `uid` — the name `ls -l` and
/// `id` print for a number.
///
/// PURE. Two names may share an id; the first line wins.
///
/// Rows: `none` when no user line carries that id — also for an id
/// whose account the host keeps outside the file, which on macOS is the
/// id of every ordinary user (`id` and `ls -l` print the NUMBER then).
/// Never traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let text = "root:x:0:0:root:/root:/bin/sh\nwolf:x:1000:1000::/home/wolf:/bin/sh\n"
/// let u = users.user_by_id(text, 0)?
/// u.name == "root"
/// ```
pub fn user_by_id(text: str, uid: int) -> User ! {none} {
    for u in users(text) {
        if u.uid == uid {
            return u
        }
    }
    return none
}

/// The FIRST group in `text` named `name` — what `chgrp name` looks up.
///
/// PURE.
///
/// Rows: `none` when no group line carries that name, including a group
/// the host keeps outside the file (the module header). Never traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let g = users.group_named("root:x:0:\npack:x:2000:wolf\n", "pack")?
/// g.gid == 2000
/// ```
pub fn group_named(text: str, name: str) -> Group ! {none} {
    for g in groups(text) {
        if g.name == name {
            return g
        }
    }
    return none
}

/// The FIRST group in `text` whose id is `gid`.
///
/// PURE.
///
/// Rows: `none` when no group line carries that id, including a group
/// the host keeps outside the file (the module header). Never traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let g = users.group_by_id("root:x:0:\npack:x:2000:wolf\n", 2000)?
/// g.name == "pack"
/// ```
pub fn group_by_id(text: str, gid: int) -> Group ! {none} {
    for g in groups(text) {
        if g.gid == gid {
            return g
        }
    }
    return none
}

/// The groups user `name` belongs to, by the group file's `text`:
/// `gid` — the user's own group, from their `User.gid` — first, then
/// the id of every group that lists `name` as a member, in the file's
/// order, each id once.
///
/// This is the list a login gives a process (`initgroups`, as far as
/// the file alone decides it), and the third argument of
/// `std.process.set_ids`: `process.set_ids(u.uid, u.gid,
/// users.groups_of(group_text, u.name, u.gid))`.
///
/// **As far as the file alone decides it, and no further.** A
/// membership the host records elsewhere is not here: macOS writes no
/// local user into `/etc/group` (the directory service holds every
/// membership), so for such a user this answers `[gid]` alone while the
/// process the host started holds more (`std.env.groups()` reads
/// those). A program that drops privileges to a directory-service user
/// over this list drops them further than a login would.
///
/// Total and PURE.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let text = "root:x:0:\nwolf:x:1000:\npack:x:2000:cub,wolf\nden:x:3000:wolf\nfox:x:4000:fox\n"
/// let gs = users.groups_of(text, "wolf", 1000)
/// gs.len == 3 && gs[0] == 1000 && gs[1] == 2000 && gs[2] == 3000
/// ```
pub fn groups_of(text: str, name: str, gid: int) -> List[int] {
    var out = List[int]()
    (mut out).push(gid)
    for g in groups(text) {
        var listed = false
        for m in g.members {
            if m == name {
                listed = true
            }
        }
        var seen = false
        for have in out {
            if have == g.gid {
                seen = true
            }
        }
        if listed && !seen {
            (mut out).push(g.gid)
        }
    }
    out
}
